{"answer":"The evidence did not separate the two explanations. It was not enough to say whether the higher average delivery time came from a broad shift affecting most orders or from a small tail of very late orders pulling the mean up.

Both tested claims stayed unresolved. The broad-shift explanation had some supporting evidence, but so did the tail-driven explanation, and neither was strong enough to stand on its own. In other words, the run found signs pointing in both directions rather than a clear winner.

It tested the distribution summary, then a dataset profile, then outlier detection. The loop stopped because it ran out of evidence, not because the question was settled. What remains open is whether the increase has been sustained or was a one-off spike, and how much of the change in the mean comes from the upper tail versus the central bulk of orders."}

